speechtext:
That this house urge upon the government the necessity for sincere and complete fulfillment of all obligations assumed under the covenant of the League of Nations and especially the prohibition of direct or indirect export to an aggressor nation of all raw materials and manufactured articles necessary for war purposes:
And be it further resolved, that this house, realizing the danger and futility of sanctions unless collectively imposed and unless accompanied by a determined effort to remove the causes of war, urge that, as now provided for in the covenant of the League and the resolutions of the assembly, the Canadian government initiate proposals to achieve the more complete fulfillment of the following purposes of the covenant:
(a)	Collective reduction of armaments;
(b)	Open and collective diplomacy with reference to international disputes;
(c)	Abrogation of treaties and agreements not in accord' with the covenant;
(d)	Definite steps under article 19 of the League covenant to revise the peace treaties and to apply the principles of international justice to the solution of those economic territorial and racial problems most frequently the cause of international disputes;
(e)	Prohibition of unilateral agreements and unilateral mobilization likely to undermine the efforts of the League to establish conditions of peace;
And be it further resolved, that, unless and until, there is a sincere acceptance and application of the above by the member-nations of the league, Canada will refuse to participate in any foreign war no matter who the belligerents may be.
He said: Mr. Speaker, no person in the house realizes more than I do how unfitted I am to deal with this task with any degree of thoroughness. Even if I were adequately fitted, how limited the time is at my disposal to deal with so great a subject.
At the outset I wish to lift this whole matter out of the realm of party politics.
